---
import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import SectionIntro from "../../components/SectionIntro.astro";
import { herbs } from "../../data/herbs";
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Herb Library | HerbGuide"
  description="Browse beginner-friendly Traditional Chinese herb profiles with plain-English summaries and food therapy context."
>
  <section class="page-hero">
    <div class="shell narrow">
      <p class="eyebrow">Herb Library</p>
      <h1>Beginner-friendly herb profiles</h1>
      <p>
        Every herb has its own dedicated page. Use the library search to jump directly to
        the herb a reader wants instead of making them scroll through an oversized list.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="shell">
      <SectionIntro
        title="Search the herb library"
        text="This first version keeps search simple and fast with client-side filtering. That gives readers direct access to each herb page without needing a database or external search service."
      />
      <div class="search-panel">
        <label class="sr-only" for="herb-search">Search herbs</label>
        <input
          id="herb-search"
          type="search"
          placeholder="Search by English name, pinyin, Chinese name, tag, or use"
        />
        <p class="search-note">Start with terms like `goji`, `huang qi`, `tea`, or `digestive`.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="card-grid" id="herb-results">
        {herbs.map((herb) => (
          <article
            class="card herb-card"
            data-search={`${herb.name} ${herb.pinyin} ${herb.chinese} ${herb.summary} ${herb.tags.join(" ")} ${herb.foodUses.join(" ")}`.toLowerCase()}
          >
            <p class="eyebrow">{herb.pinyin} · {herb.chinese}</p>
            <h2><a href={`/herbs/${herb.slug}/`}>{herb.name}</a></h2>
            <p>{herb.summary}</p>
            <p class="meta-label">Category: {herb.category}</p>
            <ul class="tag-list">
              {herb.tags.slice(0, 4).map((tag) => (
                <li>{tag}</li>
              ))}
            </ul>
            <p class="card-link">
              <a href={`/herbs/${herb.slug}/`}>Open herb page</a>
            </p>
          </article>
        ))}
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section section-alt">
    <div class="shell two-up">
      <article class="prose-block card">
        <p class="eyebrow">How to use this library</p>
        <h2>Browse Chinese herbs by name, use, and traditional category</h2>
        <p>
          This herb library is built for readers who want a beginner-friendly way to explore
          Chinese herbs without getting lost in untranslated terminology. Each herb page
          includes the English name, pinyin, Chinese characters, Latin name, traditional
          nature and flavor, channel entry, food use ideas where appropriate, and a visible
          safety note.
        </p>
        <p>
          The easiest way to use the library is to search by the word you already know.
          Some readers search by English name such as goji berry or ginseng. Others search
          by pinyin such as Huang Qi, Dang Shen, or Bai He. Many also search by broad goals
          like digestive, tea, soup, warming, cooling, or food therapy.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="prose-block card">
        <p class="eyebrow">What makes this different</p>
        <h2>Traditional Chinese herb profiles in plain English</h2>
        <p>
          Many herb sites either oversimplify Chinese herbs into marketing slogans or bury
          readers under technical jargon. HerbGuide takes a middle path. We keep the
          traditional framework visible, but we explain the terms carefully and avoid
          overstated promises.
        </p>
        <p>
          That makes this page useful for readers searching for Chinese herbs explained in
          English, how to understand pinyin herb names, or where to start with food-friendly
          Chinese herbs before moving into more advanced material.
        </p>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="shell two-up">
      <article class="prose-block card">
        <p class="eyebrow">Reader questions</p>
        <h2>Herb library FAQ</h2>
        <h3>Can I search by Chinese name or pinyin?</h3>
        <p>
          Yes. The search box is designed to work with English names, pinyin, Chinese
          characters, tags, and common food uses.
        </p>
        <h3>Which herbs are best for beginners?</h3>
        <p>
          Goji berry, fresh ginger, jujube, chrysanthemum, black sesame, and lotus seed are
          among the easiest herbs to understand because they sit close to everyday food use.
        </p>
        <h3>Are all herbs here meant for self-use?</h3>
        <p>
          No. Some pages are food-friendly and beginner-oriented, while others are included
          for educational depth and should be read more cautiously.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="prose-block card">
        <p class="eyebrow">Suggested paths</p>
        <h2>Best places to start in the herb library</h2>
        <p>
          If you want kitchen-friendly herbs first, start with
          <a href="/herbs/goji-berry/">Goji Berry</a>,
          <a href="/herbs/fresh-ginger/">Fresh Ginger</a>,
          <a href="/herbs/jujube-red-date/">Jujube</a>, and
          <a href="/herbs/chrysanthemum-flower/">Chrysanthemum</a>.
        </p>
        <p>
          If you want to understand classic tonic herbs, compare
          <a href="/herbs/astragalus-root/">Astragalus Root</a>,
          <a href="/herbs/ginseng/">Ginseng</a>, and
          <a href="/herbs/codonopsis/">Codonopsis</a>.
        </p>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>

  <script is:inline>
    const searchInput = document.querySelector("#herb-search");
    const herbCards = Array.from(document.querySelectorAll(".herb-card"));

    if (searchInput && herbCards.length > 0) {
      searchInput.addEventListener("input", (event) => {
        const value = event.target.value.trim().toLowerCase();

        herbCards.forEach((card) => {
          const haystack = card.dataset.search ?? "";
          card.style.display = haystack.includes(value) ? "" : "none";
        });
      });
    }
  </script>
</BaseLayout>
